{
  "esm": "0.8.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "advection_1d_nonuniform_dirichlet",
    "description": "MMS driver: 1-D linear advection-diffusion u_t = -c D(u,x) + nu D(D(u,x),x) on the unit interval [0, 1] with fixed-value (DIRICHLET) walls, on a SMOOTHLY STRETCHED non-uniform mesh. The advective gradient term D(u,x) is discretized by grids/cartesian_nonuniform_1d/rules/central_D1_nonuniform_dirichlet_bc (2nd-order centered nonuniform interior, second-order fixed-value wall closures) -- the RULE UNDER TEST -- and the diffusive term D(D(u,x),x) by grids/cartesian_nonuniform_1d/rules/central_D2_nonuniform_dirichlet_bc (conservative finite-volume Dirichlet Laplacian); both closures share the same wall values gL, gR. WHY ADVECTION-DIFFUSION, NOT PURE ADVECTION: a fixed-value wall closure PINS the field at a wall, which is well posed and dissipative only where a genuine second-derivative (diffusion) operator makes Dirichlet its natural condition. A pure first-order advection with fixed values at BOTH walls is ill posed -- at an outflow wall the pinned value reflects (a Kreiss/GKS-unstable mode whose L2 error GROWS as the grid refines), so no pure-advection driver converges (unlike the zero-gradient sibling advection_1d_nonuniform_zero_grad, whose extrapolation-like one-sided faces are non-reflecting). Adding a physical diffusion nu D(D(u,x),x) supplies the dissipation that renders the two-sided fixed-value problem well posed and the semi-discrete operator stable, while the advective gradient term is still genuinely exercised: a broken/first-order central_D1_nonuniform_dirichlet_bc would degrade the observed order below 2, so the sweep is a real order test of the rule. EXACT UNFORCED SOLUTION (no manufactured forcing; mirrors the parabolic-decay structure of problems/advection_1d_dirichlet_central on the uniform grid): u*(x,t) = e^{2x} + e^{-lambda t} e^{x} sin(pi x) with c = 1, nu = 1/2 and lambda = nu pi^2 + c^2/(4 nu) = 5.434802200544679. Both parts solve u_t = -c u_x + nu u_xx exactly: (i) the steady mode u_s(x) = e^{2x} = e^{(c/nu) x} satisfies -c u_s' + nu u_s'' = e^{2x}(-2 + 2) = 0 and carries the NONZERO wall values gL = u_s(0) = 1 and gR = u_s(1) = e^2 = 7.38905609893065, exercising the inhomogeneous wall term at BOTH walls; (ii) the decay part e^{-lambda t} e^{x} sin(pi x) is the first Dirichlet eigenfunction of the advection-diffusion operator (it vanishes at x=0 and x=1, leaving the wall values unchanged), driving the time evolution through which the operators are exercised. The discrete field differs from u* only by the spatial truncation of the two operators, dominated by the advective central_D1_nonuniform_dirichlet_bc term, and falls at the supraconvergent order 2 in L2. OBSERVATION WINDOW t=0.05: the diffusion is dissipative so no short-window trick is needed; t is kept modest so the decay part (e^{-lambda t} = 0.76 at t=0.05) is still O(1), keeping the dynamics active. The mesh follows the grid's EDGE-ARRAY contract (grids/cartesian_nonuniform_1d/grid.esm): the consumer supplies ONE node array xe (shape [x_nodes], N+1 values) for the quadratic stretch x(xi) = 0.6 xi + 0.4 xi^2 on uniform computational nodes xi_k = (k-1)/N, i.e. xe[k] = 0.6 ((k-1)/N) + 0.4 ((k-1)/N)^2 (xe[1]=0, xe[N+1]=1), and the GRID derives the cell centers xc via nonuniform_cell_center and the control widths dxw via nonuniform_cell_width (both rules read xc for the centered spacings and dxw for the control widths / center-to-wall half-widths). A loader-API rebinding of N re-sizes x_nodes and re-derives the whole mesh consistently. The initial condition and test references spell the stretched cell center xc[i] = (xe(i-1)+xe(i))/2 inline from literals and the metaparameter name N alone (the build-time 6.6.5/ic cellwise evaluation is scope-free; AGENTS.md build-time scope caveat; gL/gR survive only in the runtime rule bodies), algebraically identical to the grid-derived xc, so the t=0 assertion is exact.",
    "license": "MIT",
    "tags": [
      "esd:problem",
      "grid:cartesian_nonuniform_1d",
      "equation:advection_diffusion",
      "mms"
    ],
    "references": [
      {
        "citation": "Roache, P. J. (2002). Code verification by the method of manufactured solutions. J. Fluids Eng. 124(1), 4-10.",
        "doi": "10.1115/1.1436090"
      },
      {
        "citation": "Gustafsson, B., Kreiss, H.-O., Sundstrom, A. (1972). Stability theory of difference approximations for mixed initial boundary value problems. II. Mathematics of Computation 26(119), 649-686.",
        "doi": "10.1090/S0025-5718-1972-0341888-3"
      },
      {
        "citation": "LeVeque, R. J. (2007). Finite Difference Methods for Ordinary and Partial Differential Equations. SIAM. Sections 2.12 (cell-centered Dirichlet), 2.13 (nonuniform grids / supraconvergence) and Chapter 10 (well-posed boundary conditions for advection and advection-diffusion)."
      }
    ]
  },
  "models": {
    "Advection": {
      "expression_template_imports": [
        {
          "ref": "../grids/cartesian_nonuniform_1d/rules/central_D1_nonuniform_dirichlet_bc.esm"
        },
        {
          "ref": "../grids/cartesian_nonuniform_1d/rules/central_D2_nonuniform_dirichlet_bc.esm"
        }
      ],
      "variables": {
        "u": {
          "type": "state",
          "units": "kg/m^3",
          "shape": [
            "x"
          ],
          "description": "Advected-diffused tracer field over the x index set."
        },
        "c": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "m/s",
          "default": 1.0,
          "description": "Constant transport speed for the advective gradient term D(u,x) (the rule under test). Fixes the steady mode e^{(c/nu)x} and the eigenfunction skew e^{cx/(2nu)}."
        },
        "nu": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "m^2/s",
          "default": 0.5,
          "description": "Diffusivity for the stabilizing D(D(u,x),x) term. Makes the two-sided fixed-value problem well posed; c/nu = 2 keeps advection a leading contributor to the truncation."
        },
        "gL": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "kg/m^3",
          "default": 1.0,
          "description": "Prescribed field value at the left wall x=xe[1], shared by the advective (central_D1_nonuniform_dirichlet_bc) and diffusive (central_D2_nonuniform_dirichlet_bc) Dirichlet closures. Set to 1 = u_s(0) = e^0 (the rules' own free-name default is 0; this problem overrides it nonzero so the inhomogeneous wall term appears at runtime)."
        },
        "gR": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "kg/m^3",
          "default": 7.38905609893065,
          "description": "Prescribed field value at the right wall x=xe[N+1], shared by both Dirichlet closures. Set to e^2 = 7.38905609893065 = u_s(1) (the rules' own free-name default is 0; this problem overrides it nonzero so the inhomogeneous wall term appears at runtime)."
        },
        "xe": {
          "type": "observed",
          "units": "m",
          "shape": [
            "x_nodes"
          ],
          "description": "Node (edge) coordinates of the quadratic-stretch mesh, xe[k] = 0.6 ((k-1)/N) + 0.4 ((k-1)/N)^2 for node k = 1..N+1 (xe[1]=0, xe[N+1]=1): the grid's single geometry input over the N+1-member node axis x_nodes. N is the document-scoped metaparameter substituted at load (esm-spec 9.7.6), so a convergence-sweep rebinding of N re-derives the nodes. Aggregation index `k` over x_nodes.",
          "expression": {
            "op": "aggregate",
            "output_idx": [
              "k"
            ],
            "args": [],
            "ranges": {
              "k": {
                "from": "x_nodes"
              }
            },
            "expr": {
              "op": "+",
              "args": [
                {
                  "op": "*",
                  "args": [
                    0.6,
                    {
                      "op": "/",
                      "args": [
                        {
                          "op": "-",
                          "args": [
                            "k",
                            1
                          ]
                        },
                        "N"
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "op": "*",
                  "args": [
                    0.4,
                    {
                      "op": "/",
                      "args": [
                        {
                          "op": "-",
                          "args": [
                            "k",
                            1
                          ]
                        },
                        "N"
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "op": "/",
                      "args": [
                        {
                          "op": "-",
                          "args": [
                            "k",
                            1
                          ]
                        },
                        "N"
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        },
        "xc": {
          "type": "observed",
          "units": "m",
          "shape": [
            "x"
          ],
          "description": "Cell centers, GRID-DERIVED from xe by applying grids/cartesian_nonuniform_1d nonuniform_cell_center (xc[i] = (xe[i]+xe[i+1])/2). Read by both rule bodies for the center-to-center stencil spacings.",
          "expression": {
            "op": "apply_expression_template",
            "args": [],
            "name": "nonuniform_cell_center",
            "bindings": {
              "xe": "xe"
            }
          }
        },
        "dxw": {
          "type": "observed",
          "units": "m",
          "shape": [
            "x"
          ],
          "description": "Finite-volume control widths, GRID-DERIVED from xe by applying nonuniform_cell_width (dxw[i] = xe[i+1]-xe[i]). Read by the diffusive Laplacian as the control-cell divisor and by both Dirichlet closures, halved, as the center-to-wall distance dxw[1]/2, dxw[N]/2. Because both xc and dxw descend from the same xe they are automatically consistent.",
          "expression": {
            "op": "apply_expression_template",
            "args": [],
            "name": "nonuniform_cell_width",
            "bindings": {
              "xe": "xe"
            }
          }
        }
      },
